y been fired from his job as a  police officer after he was caught with  methamphetamines Thailand's prime  minister has responded to the attacks by  ordering a strict clampdown on drugs and  drug dealers as well as tightening gun  control rules  ahead the age Gap in cancer treatment  why some young Canadians face huge  hurdles accessing life-saving  medications  when a young Canadian is diagnosed with  cancer the cost of treatment should be  the last thing on their mind but when it  comes to accessing life-saving drugs in  Canada it turns out age does matter  because there's no National Drug  insurance plan those without private  coverage are at the mercy of provincial  rules and as Jamie Maraca explains the  disparity can put patients in expensive  life or death situations  32 year old Rebecca Grundy is beating  the odds it's almost four years to the  day where I woke up uh on a stretcher in  a hospital hallway after suffering from  three Grandma seizures the cause a grade  four glioblastoma an extremely  aggressive form of brain cancer with one  of the lowest survival rates typically  less than 18 months it's most commonly  seen in adults over the age of 45.  Grundy was just 28 when she found out  about her tumor and in Ontario the drugs  she needed to survive an at-home  treatment called tamazolamide isn't  covered for patients her age I would  have to jump through all of these hoops  as a young person under the age of 65 to  get access to this treatment that is  supposed to kill the cancer cells and  save my life and that's just not fair  Grundy spent weeks waiting for  provincial assistance time many of these  patients don't have that's the most  devastating situation when the most  effective the most convenient the best  tolerated treatment just no longer  becomes an option and we have to use an  inferior treatment with more toxicities  in Atlantic provinces patients face a  similar Patchwork of drug coverage  meanwhile in Quebec private or public  insurance 